note: chat.q:215:1 — N_NEVER_COMPLETE: query chat.groupByTitle: this lookup does not pin an authorized row's id; matching a name does not prove uniqueness or absence. The server may return matches but cannot certify complete. The held replacement (check --verbose) keeps the same matches and shape on the device; it requires acquired coverage and has no server fallback. Keep the original query if server lookup is required
  rewrite: query groupByTitle(title: text 1..50) held:
  return conversations where .title = title and .kind = 'group' first 1 by .id
  guide: reads
  why: snapback4 why N_NEVER_COMPLETE

N_NEVER_COMPLETE: a note, not an error — a page whose read rule reads a column the scan does not pin is complete: false on the server whatever it finds. Pin that column with a where equality. For membership search, use messages matching q where exists members[.roomId, viewer] last 20 by .at after c; or read held rows on the device. guide reads, search.

# Reads

The first read examples use the language card's tables. A page's read rule is checked
once when the prefix decides it, else per row, and a page whose rule still depends
on a column the scan does not pin is never `complete` (N_NEVER_COMPLETE names
the missing equality columns and an existing compatible index when one is
available). Keep the current equality prefix when adding those pins; choosing
a conversation narrows the answer to that scope. This is author guidance,
not a `check --fix` repair, and it does not change the read rule. With a total unique `(chatKey, principal)`
key on t, `.principal = viewer or exists t[.chatKey, viewer]` is decided by
the chat alone because the row is its own witness, so a roster page pinned
by the chat is complete and no second table is needed. An aborting mutation
page costs one examined unit before its verdict, plus its rule probes on
both true and false verdicts; a true verdict then examines candidates.
A page verdict evaluated from its pinned prefix charges the rule's static probe count on either outcome, including probes skipped by `and`/`or` short-circuiting.
A point read reserves that same static allowance before its lookup, equally
for a readable row, a hidden row and a miss. If it cannot pay, authorization
has no verdict and the answer remains incomplete. A device's trusted held
row needs no authorization probes.
A read states its predicate, search text, page size, order and continuation:
`t where predicate matching q last 20 by .at after c`. Omit parts the read
does not need. `first` ascends; `last` descends; id breaks ties. Predicates run
after the source rule. Parenthesize a nested read with its own modifiers:
`exists (messages[roomId = .roomId] matching q)`. The named inner key binds
the outer row's room; inside a predicate, `.` denotes that predicate's row.

A lookup by title does not prove that title unique. Do not add a uniqueness
constraint unless the product requires it, or pin an id you do not have.
For a synced table whose read rule depends on its id, the note can print the
same complete query declaration with `held` added. Paste it over that query
only if device-only lookup fits the screen: its predicate, order and duplicate
matches stay the same, but it requires acquired coverage and has no server
fallback. Otherwise keep the server query and its incomplete absence verdict.

Full card: snapback4 guide reads

note: schema.q:81:1 — N_MAINTAIN_SYNCED: 2 synced maintains:
  maintain replyCounts: sync public inherited from posts, no horizon; one value row per parentId reaches every device; no currently eligible mutation reads it (site schema.q:81:1)
  maintain repostCounts: sync public inherited from reposts, no horizon; one value row per postId reaches every device; no currently eligible mutation reads it (site schema.q:80:1)
  rewrite: keep inherited sync for local readers and offline absence coverage, or add `online only` and lose that offline coverage; measure cold rows with `snapback4 test --residency`; guide maintains; guide offline-interactions
  guide: residency
  why: snapback4 why N_MAINTAIN_SYNCED

N_MAINTAIN_SYNCED: maintains inherit sync with no horizon: public or a group audience with unbounded recipients or multiple values per group. One grouped note retains every source site and names mutations that stop predicting with online only, which loses offline aggregate/absence coverage. snapback4 test --residency measures cold rows.

## Operations

N_MAINTAIN_SYNCED names public inherited aggregates and group audiences
with unbounded recipients or multiple values per group. It names mutations
that would stop predicting if the view goes online only. Inherited sync has no
horizon; `online only` also loses offline aggregate/absence coverage. See
`guide maintains` and `guide offline-interactions` for the replica census. Repeats share one
note with a line and source location for every view. Check prints these
facts without pre-adoption counts: a new view has no stored rows yet. Run
`test --residency --fixture dir --as alice` to measure cold rows and compact
JSON row bytes. The default report shows the eight costliest table/persona
pairs by bytes (then rows), one summed remainder and reconciled totals.
`check --residency --fixture dir --as alice` prints the same bounded report.
Without a fixture, check measures the active dev-store schema read-only;
it never adopts current source for measurement. With no store it says not
measured and names the fixture command. Add `--verbose` for every table,
facts and notes (and on test, query meters and aggregate row counts).
Measurements exclude transport framing, are not process RSS, and are not
estimates for another fixture.

Full card: snapback4 guide residency

note: chat.q:352:1 — N_MAINTAIN_DELTA: maintain unread may disclose the delta of a hidden messages row: insert/delete and a potentially wider update rule affect this readable target; filter/key/value dependencies: conversation, ordinal, sender, tombstone
  rewrite: treat the view as a declared read channel; restrict its read rule or the source update rule if the delta is private
  guide: rules
  why: snapback4 why N_MAINTAIN_DELTA

N_MAINTAIN_DELTA: a readable aggregate view declares an insert/delete/update delta channel, including filter/key/value dependencies; restrict its read audience or source write authority if the delta is private.

# Rules

A hidden collision on ordinary insert or `insert ... if absent` stays
`E_RULE`; a readable collision names its index (if-absent returns null).
An update or upsert-patch colliding with a different hidden row returns generic
`E_RULE` when its target is readable and `NOT_FOUND` when its target is unreadable.
Use `upsert counters[recipient] { count: 1 }` under an `update` rule to
write another principal's row. Success discloses the admitted row's existence
and the update rule predicates. It grants no extra stream or snapshot row.
A condition belongs on `update`; an upsert inserts or patches. A condition
needs a row you can read; a blind patch needs none. On a row you cannot read,
immutable columns in a patch are ignored. A readable immutable mismatch still
names the column. Before target lookup, the engine reserves room for the rule
probes and the largest merged image allowed by the schema and supplied patch;
if it cannot fit, `E_BOUND` refuses equally for a hidden or absent target.
A readable aggregate view over a hidden source is a declared read channel: its
delta can disclose the old contribution. `N_MAINTAIN_DELTA` notes potentially
wider update authority under a separately readable aggregate view. Restrict its
read audience or source write authority if that delta is private.

Full card: snapback4 guide rules

note: schema.q:43:3 — N_MAINTAINED_COLUMN_STREAM: posts: each contributing commit logs and streams the whole parent image per destination partition, coalesced once per row; a 4,000-byte parent costs every holder about 4 KB per change
  rewrite: use a column when readers want the count with the row; for a large row or a counter changing far more often than it is read, use a view with online only
  guide: maintains
  why: snapback4 why N_MAINTAINED_COLUMN_STREAM

N_MAINTAINED_COLUMN_STREAM: each contributing commit streams the whole parent image per destination partition. Consider the parent's size and update frequency; a hot counter can stay a separate online-only view. guide maintains.

## Maintained columns

A contributing commit streams the whole parent image to each parent
partition, coalesced once per row. N_MAINTAINED_COLUMN_STREAM names that cost
on the parent's sync line. Use a column for a count readers want with the
row; use `view …` with `online only` when the counter changes far more often
than the row is read. Include the parent's size in that choice: a 4,000-byte
parent costs every holder about 4 KB per contributing commit.

Full card: snapback4 guide maintains

note: chat.q:320:10 — N_SYNC_ORDER: query chat.inChat `messages[conversation = conversation] matching q last 20 by .at after c` reads messages by .at, .id, newest first (index [conversation, at, id]); its sync window is last 500 by .ordinal, .id, newest first (index byConversationOrdinal [conversation, ordinal]); the client needs the server for this read; offline coverage is unknown
  rewrite: The window uses .ordinal, .id, newest first (index byConversationOrdinal [conversation, ordinal]); preserve all predicates, audience keys and the cursor. No paste-ready replacement is available for this read; retain its enclosing row or merge context
  guide: sync
  why: snapback4 why N_SYNC_ORDER

N_SYNC_ORDER: a note, not an error — a device-capable query pages a windowed table on another index (including a calendar index) or oldest first. Whole-domain aggregates and word indexes have separate coverage requirements and are outside this ordering advice. Its acquisition boundary proves only newest-first pages on the window's own index, within that boundary. The note names both orders and the source site. A paste-ready page uses last N on the window index, audience equalities in bracket keys, and the original predicates and cursor. It requires a cursor, a cap covering the whole group, or the same ordered columns already read newest first. A cursorless partial page in another order gets words because reordering could select different rows. Restart pagination when changing order; the full traversal keeps the same rows. Only scope-independent range endpoints can move into residual filters. Outer-row endpoints, generated membership lanes, explicit merges and projected views get the window order and index in words without a paste-ready replacement; one lane is not the whole read. Capped pages get last N from their bound scan. Otherwise the ordinary query needs the server; offline coverage is unknown. A held query stays on the device and reports incomplete coverage without server fallback. online only currently applies to tables and views, not query headers. guide sync.

# Sync: the viewer's partition is the unit of sync

Ascending reads and other indexes have unknown coverage.
N_SYNC_ORDER notes these query orders at their source sites and names the
window's order for ordinary pages, including calendar-index pages. A printed
page repair keeps audience equalities in bracket keys, every other predicate,
and the cursor. It is printed only when the query has a cursor, a cap proves
the page covers the whole group, or the query already reads newest first on
the same ordered columns. A cursorless partial page in another order gets
words: changing its order could select different rows. After changing the
order, restart pagination; the full traversal keeps the same rows.
A window index fully pinned by its audience also gets words: its point read
would change the page's result type.
A range can move to a filter only with the same meaning;
outer-row endpoints, generated membership lanes and merges get words naming
the window order and index instead of a paste-ready replacement. This also
applies to projected views: one branch is not the whole result. Whole-domain
aggregates and word searches have separate coverage requirements; this
ordering note does not cover them.
Use `last N by .at, .id` for the example above, keeping the
audience predicate and cursor. A query on the window's own index, newest first,
can answer offline within the acquired boundary, including timestamp ties.
Other orders need the server; a held query stays local with incomplete coverage.
`online only` currently applies to tables and views, not query headers.

Full card: snapback4 guide sync

